from science_completion import q,p,m

QUESTIONS=[
q(1,'Name the wave type in which vibrations are parallel to the direction of energy transfer.','Longitudinal.','4.6.1.1'),
q(1,'What does the amplitude of a wave measure?','The maximum displacement from the resting position.','4.6.1.2'),
q(1,'Give the approximate upper frequency limit of normal human hearing.','20 kHz.','4.6.1.4'),
q(1,'Which electromagnetic group lies between visible light and X-rays?','Ultraviolet.','4.6.2.1'),
q(1,'What type of image can be formed on a screen?','Real.','4.6.2.5'),
q(1,'Name the line from which angles of incidence and reflection are measured.','The normal.','4.6.1.3','2022 2H Q02.3; 2023 2H Q07.2'),
q(1,'Which seismic wave cannot travel through liquids?','S-wave.','4.6.1.5'),
q(1,'What happens to a wave’s frequency when it enters a different medium?','It stays the same.','4.6.1.2'),
q(1,'What is a perfect black body?','An object that absorbs all radiation incident on it.','4.6.3.1'),
q(2,'Compare particle vibrations in transverse and longitudinal waves.','- Transverse vibrations are **perpendicular** to energy transfer.\n- Longitudinal vibrations are **parallel** to energy transfer.','4.6.1.1'),
q(2,'A cork bobs in a ripple tank but stays in approximately the same horizontal position. Explain what this shows.','- The cork and nearby water move locally as the disturbance passes.\n- The wave transfers **energy without an overall transfer of the water** across the tank.','4.6.1.1'),
q(2,'A wave has frequency 25 Hz. Calculate its period.','**T = 1/f = 1/25 = 0.040 s**.','4.6.1.2'),
q(2,'A ripple has frequency 5.0 Hz and wavelength 0.060 m. Calculate its speed.','**v = fλ = 5.0 × 0.060 = 0.30 m/s**.','4.6.1.2'),
q(2,'Why is a distance–displacement wave graph unsuitable for reading the period directly?','- Its horizontal axis shows **distance**, so a full cycle gives wavelength.\n- Period needs a **time axis** or a separate timing measurement.','4.6.1.2'),
q(2,'A ray strikes a mirror at 35° to the normal. State its reflection angle and its angle to the mirror surface.','- Reflection angle = **35°**.\n- Angle to the surface = 90° − 35° = **55°**.','4.6.1.3','2022 2H Q02.3; 2023 2H Q07.2'),
q(2,'Explain why rough surfaces scatter reflected light even though the reflection law still applies.','- Different small parts of the surface have **different normal directions**.\n- Each obeys i = r, but the reflected rays travel in **different directions**.','4.6.2.6','2023 2H Q07.1: contrasting reflection type'),
q(2,'Why can ultrasound be used to find a hidden boundary?','- Some ultrasound is **reflected at a boundary between different materials**.\n- The returning echo’s time delay reveals the boundary’s **distance**, using the wave speed.','4.6.1.5'),
q(2,'Explain why S-wave observations support a liquid outer core.','- S-waves **cannot travel through liquids**.\n- Their failure to pass through the outer core supports the conclusion that it is **liquid**.','4.6.1.5'),
q(2,'Why are X-rays suitable for an image of a broken bone?','- Bone **absorbs more X-rays** than surrounding soft tissue.\n- Different amounts reaching the detector produce **contrast**, showing the bone and fracture.','4.6.2.4'),
q(2,'Explain why a red shirt appears dark under only blue light.','- It mostly **absorbs blue** and reflects red.\n- There is little red in the illumination, so **little light reaches the eye from the shirt**.','4.6.2.6'),
q(2,'A lens forms a 30 mm image of a 12 mm object. Calculate magnification and state whether it has a unit.','Magnification = 30/12 = **2.5**, with **no unit**.','4.6.2.5'),
q(2,'Explain why an object cools by radiation when it emits 80 W and absorbs 50 W.','- It loses energy at a **net rate of 30 W**.\n- More radiation is emitted than absorbed, so its **temperature falls**, assuming no other transfers balance the loss.','4.6.3.2'),
q(2,'Why must the distance between an infrared detector and each Leslie-cube face be the same?','- Detector reading changes with distance.\n- Keeping distance constant helps ensure differences are caused by **surface finish**, rather than measurement position.','4.6.2.2','2023 2H Q01: infrared investigation'),
q(2,'Two students report reflection angles of 29.1° and 29.2° using a protractor marked every 1°. Explain the problem.','- The instrument’s **resolution is 1°**.\n- It does not resolve a **0.1° difference**, so those readings imply unjustified precision.','4.6.1.3','2023 2H Q07.5'),
q(2,'How can radio waves be generated and then detected in electrical circuits?','- **Oscillating electrical currents** generate radio waves.\n- Absorbed radio waves can induce an **alternating current at the same frequency** in a receiving circuit.','4.6.2.3'),
q(3,'Sound of frequency 400 Hz travels at 340 m/s in air and 1,500 m/s in water. Calculate its wavelength in both media and explain the frequency.','- Air: λ = v/f = 340/400 = **0.85 m**.\n- Water: λ = 1,500/400 = **3.75 m**.\n- Frequency remains **400 Hz** because it is set by the source.','4.6.1.2'),
q(3,'A microwave travels at 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s and has wavelength 12.0 cm. Calculate its frequency in standard form.','- λ = **0.120 m**.\n- f = v/λ = (3.0 × 10⁸)/0.120.\n- **f = 2.5 × 10⁹ Hz**.','4.6.1.2','2023 2H Q06.2: adapted wave-equation conversion'),
q(3,'An echo returns 0.060 s after a pulse is sent through water. Sound travels at 1,480 m/s. Calculate the distance to the reflector.','- Total travelled distance = vt = 1,480 × 0.060 = **88.8 m**.\n- This is the **out-and-back** journey.\n- Distance to reflector = 88.8/2 = **44.4 m**.','4.6.1.5'),
q(3,'Explain why a light ray entering glass obliquely bends towards the normal.','- Parts of a wavefront reach the glass at **different times**.\n- Light travels **more slowly in glass**, so one part slows before another.\n- The wavefront turns, changing the ray’s direction **towards the normal**.','4.6.2.2','2023 2H Q07.6'),
q(3,'A convex lens is used as a magnifying glass. Where is the object, and what are three properties of its image?','- Object is **closer to the lens than its focal length**.\n- Image is **virtual and upright**.\n- It is **magnified**, on the same side as the object.','4.6.2.5'),
q(3,'Describe how to locate the virtual image formed by a concave lens on a ray diagram.','- Draw a ray from the object tip **parallel to the axis**, then diverging as if from the near focus.\n- Draw a second ray through the **optical centre**, continuing straight.\n- Extend outgoing rays **backwards with dashed lines**. Their intersection locates the virtual image tip.','4.6.2.5'),
q(3,'Explain one benefit and two precautions when using X-rays for medical imaging.','- They can reveal internal structures such as **bone fractures** without surgery.\n- Use the **lowest dose that gives useful information**, limiting exposure time and unnecessary repeats.\n- Use appropriate **shielding** for staff and unaffected areas where suitable, because ionising radiation can damage cells.','4.6.2.3–4.6.2.4'),
q(3,'Six consecutive crest lines span 0.15 m in a ripple tank. Frequency is 12 Hz. Calculate wavelength and speed.','- Six crest lines contain **five wavelengths**.\n- λ = 0.15/5 = **0.030 m**.\n- v = fλ = 12 × 0.030 = **0.36 m/s**.','4.6.1.2'),
q(3,'A string has four stationary loops across 1.20 m and vibrates at 50 Hz. Calculate the travelling wave’s wavelength and speed.','- Each loop spans **half a wavelength**.\n- λ = 2 × 1.20/4 = **0.60 m**.\n- v = 50 × 0.60 = **30 m/s**.','4.6.1.2'),
q(3,'White light passes through an ideal red filter and then an ideal blue filter. Explain why almost no light emerges.','- The first filter **transmits red light** and absorbs other visible wavelengths.\n- The second mainly **transmits blue** and absorbs red.\n- Little of the light leaving the first filter can pass the second; filters **do not create new colours**.','4.6.2.6'),
q(4,'Describe a method to measure the speed of sound using two microphones.','- Measure the **separation** of microphones connected to a data logger or oscilloscope.\n- Make a sharp sound in line with them and identify the **same pulse** in both recordings.\n- Measure arrival-time difference and calculate **v = separation/time delay**.\n- Repeat and average. Use enough separation for a measurable delay; electronic timing avoids human reaction-time error.','4.6.1.2'),
q(4,'Explain how a warming object’s emitted radiation changes and what radiation balance means.','- It emits **more energy per second** as temperature rises.\n- Its spectrum shifts towards **shorter wavelengths**.\n- At constant temperature, absorbed and emitted power balance if radiation is the only energy transfer.\n- If absorbed power exceeds emitted power, its **temperature rises** until the balance changes.','4.6.3.1–4.6.3.2'),
q(4,'An IR experiment compares a black and a shiny surface. The black surface starts hotter and is closer to the detector. Explain why the result does not isolate the effect of surface finish and how to improve it.','- **Hotter surfaces emit more**, so starting-temperature differences affect the readings.\n- **Distance** also affects the detector’s reading.\n- Use faces at the **same temperature**, for example on one hot Leslie cube.\n- Keep **detector distance and angle fixed**, repeat, and compare readings.','4.6.2.2–4.6.3.1','2023 2H Q01: fair comparison'),
q(4,'Describe how a convex-lens ray diagram shows a real image for an object beyond twice the focal length.','- Draw the axis, lens, **F and 2F** on both sides, and the object beyond 2F.\n- Draw a tip ray parallel to the axis, refracted through the **far focus**.\n- Draw a second ray through the optical centre, continuing straight.\n- Their intersection gives a **real, inverted, smaller** image between F and 2F.','4.6.2.5'),
q(6,'Describe a valid investigation of how incident angle affects refraction through a rectangular glass block. Include measurement and ways to reduce uncertainty.','- Place the block on paper and **trace its outline** so its position is recorded.\n- Shine a **narrow ray** at one face. Mark two points on the incident ray and two on the emerging ray while the block stays fixed.\n- Remove it. Join the ray marks and connect entry and exit points to reconstruct the path inside the glass.\n- Draw a **normal at 90° to the surface** at entry. Measure incidence and refraction angles from this normal using a protractor.\n- Repeat across a suitable range of incident angles, keeping the same block and entry face. Repeat readings and calculate means.\n- Use fine marks and a ruler, consistent eye position, and a resolution appropriate to the claimed difference. Plot refracted angle against incident angle and describe the trend. Do not replace a refraction method with a mirror-reflection method.','4.6.1.3–4.6.2.2','2022 2H Q02.1–02.4; 2023 2H Q07.3–07.6'),
q(6,'Describe how to compare the infrared absorption of different surface finishes. Explain how to make a valid comparison.','- Use otherwise identical containers or samples with different finishes. Put the **same mass of water or material** in each, starting at the same temperature.\n- Place each at the **same distance and orientation** from an IR lamp, with equal exposure time and lamp output.\n- Record initial and final temperatures and calculate **temperature increases**. Keep thermometer positions, room conditions and other apparatus comparable.\n- Repeat, average and compare the rises. A greater rise under matched conditions indicates greater absorption, because the same amount of material needs more absorbed energy to warm further.\n- Prevent unequal mass, shading, draughts or differing heat loss from determining the result. A matt black surface generally absorbs more IR than a shiny surface.\n- Use heatproof surfaces and avoid hot lamps or water; keep electrical equipment dry.','4.6.2.2','2023 2H Q01.4–01.5'),
q(6,'Explain how a change in atmospheric greenhouse gases can affect Earth’s temperature. Include incoming, outgoing and reflected radiation.','- Solar radiation reaches Earth; **some is reflected** into space and some is absorbed by the surface and atmosphere.\n- Earth’s surface **emits infrared**, transferring energy away.\n- Greenhouse gases **absorb and emit infrared**, including emission towards the surface, changing energy transfer to space.\n- An increase can initially reduce outgoing energy for the existing temperature. Then absorbed incoming power exceeds outgoing power and the surface and lower atmosphere **warm**.\n- As temperature rises, radiation emitted increases. A new balance can be reached at a higher temperature, depending on the other factors involved.\n- Changes in reflection, such as cloud or surface changes, also affect absorbed sunlight. The explanation is an **energy balance**, not a claim that all radiation is trapped forever.','4.6.3.2')]

